speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I must compliment my hon. friend (Mr. Blain) on the shortness of his speech at all events and I will try and emulate him in that respect. At this late date of the session, I do not think it is necessary for us to thresh out and repeat the discussion that took place in the earlier part of the session on this question. My hon. friend has made a serious reflection upon this government on the ground that the expenses in connection with taking the census of 1901 is excessive in comparison especially with the cost of taking the census of 1891. In general terms I Mr. BLAIN.
would have to very shortly state that if the expense was very much greater the work of taking the census was also very much greater in consequence of two or three facts which nobody can dispute. The expense of doing the same amount of work in the year 1901 was more than in the year 1891. because, in the former year, the cost of labour, the cost of living and of everything in connection with all kinds of work in Canada was decidedly greater. I am not able at the moment, speaking from memory, to say what the proportionate cost of labour and living would be in each of these respective years, but, without going into the details, I am safe, I think, in saying that the tariff of rates established for the payment of the census workers in 1901 was fixed on a basis which was comparatively fair in order to give the people employment in taking the census, a fair amount of pay for the same amount of work they performed in 1891. In 1891, the enumerators of the census were paid at the rate of three cents for each name they took, three cents for each death they enumerated, fifteen cents for each farm the details of which they enumerated, and twenty cents for each factory, the details of which they enumerated. In 1901, we established the following rates : Five cents for each person enumerated, five cents for each death enumerated, twenty-five cents for each farm and thirty cents for each factory.
]Mr. HENDERSON. How much for each farm V

